    <div class="reading-top">
      <div><span class="rk">Inside the Factories Act</span><div class="big" id="rb-formal">–</div></div>
      <p class="rt-cap">Persons engaged in registered factories in 2023-24 — beside the workers of unincorporated establishments the same year. For every person inside the factory gate, about <b id="rt-ratio">–</b> work outside it, in enterprises the Act never sees.</p>
      <div><span class="rk">Outside it</span><div class="big" id="rb-informal" style="font-size:44px">–</div></div>
    </div>
    <div class="reading-cols">
      <div class="reading-col">
        <span class="mk">What the print says</span>
        <p>The 2023-24 survey found <b id="cr-fop">–</b> factories in operation, of <b id="cr-freg">–</b> on the register. They engaged <b id="cr-pers">–</b> people and produced <b id="cr-fgva">–</b> of gross value added — <b id="cr-gpwf">–</b> per person, in the rupees of that year.</p>
        <p>ASUSE's third round counted <b id="cr-est">–</b> unincorporated establishments with <b id="cr-wrk">–</b> workers, adding <b id="cr-gpwi">–</b> of value per worker per year. Set one against the other and the factory multiple is <b id="cr-mult">–</b>.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="reading-col now">
        <span class="mk">What it does not say</span>
        <p>It does not say India makes things in only two lakh places. The ASI sees factories registered under the Factories Act — broadly, premises with ten or more workers using power, or twenty without. Everything smaller is invisible to it by design, and everything smaller is most of the country.</p>
        <p>Nor do the rupee figures say "growth" by themselves. ASI values are nominal — no deflator is published for them — and the informal count has no history: ASUSE first measured it in 2021-22. Whether there were more informal establishments a decade ago, we do not know.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

