  <!-- § 08 the measure -->
  <section>
    <div class="sec-head"><span class="no">08</span><h2>What this measures</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="wm-grid">
      <div>
        <p>The ASI is a census-plus-sample of the Factories Act register: every large factory enumerated, the rest sampled, once a year, results final on release. "Registered" is the load-bearing word — broadly, ten or more workers with power, twenty without. The survey measures premises, money and people: counts of factories and workers are clean; every rupee figure is nominal, published in ₹ lakh, with no deflator offered or possible at this level.</p>
        <p>ASUSE is the successor to the old NSS enterprise surveys, run annually since 2021-22. It samples households and premises to find unincorporated establishments — no company registration, no factory licence — in manufacturing, trade and other services. Its rupee figures are per-establishment and per-worker rates, also nominal. Three rounds exist. That is the entire history of India's informal-enterprise count.</p>
        <p>Between the two surveys runs a seam, and much of the economy falls through it: agriculture, government, construction, and every incorporated firm that is not a factory — the software company, the bank, the airline. Neither survey sees them. This page describes the two shores it can see, and says so when it cannot.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="wm-rail">
        <aside class="margin-note">
          <span class="mk">Margin note · i</span>
          <b>Nominal rupees throughout.</b> No deflator is applied and none is published for ASI. Rupee levels mix quantity with fifteen years of inflation; only the counts are free of prices.
        </aside>
        <aside class="margin-note">
          <span class="mk">Margin note · ii</span>
          <b>A factory is a premises; an establishment is not an enterprise.</b> One firm can run many factories; one owner, several establishments. Neither survey counts companies.
        </aside>
        <aside class="margin-note">
          <span class="mk">Margin note · iii</span>
          <b>The 2010-11 break.</b> The ASI frame revision lifted the factory count by roughly a third in one year. Any series crossing that year carries the break; this dossier annotates it wherever it appears.
        </aside>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- § 09 misread -->
  <section>
    <div class="sec-head"><span class="no">09</span><h2>How to misread this number</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <ul class="misread">
      <li>
        <span class="no">i.</span>
        <div>
          <h3>"India has only two lakh factories."</h3>
          <p>It has two lakh <em>registered</em> factories — premises large enough for the Factories Act. Crores of manufacturing establishments operate below its thresholds, in the other survey on this page. "Factory" is a legal category, not a synonym for a place where things are made.</p>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="no">ii.</span>
        <div>
          <h3>"Factory GVA rose by double digits — manufacturing is booming."</h3>
          <p>ASI rupees are nominal. Part of any rise is the price level, and the survey publishes no deflator to remove it. For volume, lean on the counts — factories, persons — or on the deflated production indices, which are a different instrument entirely.</p>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="no">iii.</span>
        <div>
          <h3>"The factory count jumped a third in 2010-11."</h3>
          <p>The sampling frame was rebuilt that year and existing factories became visible; almost nothing was built that was not already there. Growth computed across 2010-11 is a property of the survey, not the economy. The break is annotated on every chart it touches.</p>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="no">iv.</span>
        <div>
          <h3>"Informal workers are eight times less productive."</h3>
          <p>The eight-fold gap is real, but it belongs to the establishment, not the worker. Factories carry capital, power and scale in industries a shop cannot enter; the comparison also spans different sectors doing different work. Pay gaps, the cleaner person-level signal, are far narrower.</p>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="no">v.</span>
        <div>
          <h3>"ASUSE counts the whole informal economy."</h3>
          <p>It counts unincorporated establishments in manufacturing, trade and other services. Farms are excluded, as are construction, government and anything incorporated. Informal work in those sectors exists — the labour survey sees the workers — but no survey counts those enterprises, and this page does not pretend otherwise.</p>
        </div>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <footer class="provenance">
    <div class="cols">
      <div>
        <h6>Source of record</h6>
        <p>Ministry of Statistics &amp; Programme Implementation, National Statistical Office. Annual Survey of Industries, NIC-2008 block, 2008-09 to 2023-24; ASUSE annual rounds 2021-22 to 2023-24 and quarterly bulletin from Jan–Mar 2025. Retrieved via the eSankhyiki API, vintage <b id="prov-vintage">–</b>.</p>
        <p>ASI monetary values are published in ₹ lakh and are shown here in ₹ lakh crore, nominal, with no deflation. The 2010-11 sampling-frame revision is carried as published and annotated wherever a series crosses it. Ladakh's factory GVA is negative in 2021-22 — a real published value, inputs exceeding output, not an error. Telangana appears from 2012-13 and Ladakh from 2019-20; earlier cells are null, not zero.</p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h6>Revisions &amp; uncertainty</h6>
        <p>ASI and ASUSE results are final on release. The two surveys have different frames, units and histories and are shown side by side, never spliced or summed. ASUSE 2023-24 publishes per-hired-worker figures for hired-worker establishments only; the all-establishment variants end at 2022-23 and carry a disclosed trailing null.</p>
        <p>ASUSE quarterly counts are published in hundreds and converted for display. Where something is not measured — the informal economy before 2021-22, real factory output — this dossier says "we do not know" rather than inventing a number.</p>
      </div>
      <div>
